00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:41.720
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): And I'm trying to see how parallel it is to when instant messaging was hot, and instant messaging came to the ITF.

274
00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:44.530
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Like.

275
00:46:44.820 --> 00:46:59.969
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): why did people show up? Who was afraid of missing the boat? One of the advantages of bringing something to the IETF is that, companies like, well, Cisco at the time, but it'd be different names you'd conjure today.

276
00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:03.820
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): couldn't say that they were unaware of the work. They couldn't just ignore it.

277
00:47:04.120 --> 00:47:10.249
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Because they were at the IETF anyways, going to router layer working groups.

278
00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:11.780
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): And…

279
00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:23.839
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): what Jabber did was, first of all, they built an open source thing that worked, and had a few people involved. Then they brought it to IETF and gave up control.

280
00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:30.349
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): in order to broaden the audience, because, again, Cisco couldn't say.

281
00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:35.239
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): They were unaware of it. They couldn't say it didn't work for them if they didn't put comments on it.

282
00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:38.649
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): When they were there, at the, you know, at each venue.

